    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Babur spent his youth winning and losing Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> to rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> before making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> his base in 1504.</li><li>Using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> and linked carts in the Ottoman fashion, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at Panipat in 1526 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> at Khanwa in 1527, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</li><li>His memoir, the Baburnama, frank about his own faults, records his campaigns, gardens and the violence of his conquests, and is regarded as one of the earliest true autobiographies in Islamic literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a>, Babur publicly renounced wine and had his gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> drinking vessels broken up and the fragments given to the poor.</p></aside>
    </section>

