    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1494</span><span class="rf-text">Babur becomes Timurid ruler of Fergana on the death of his father, aged eleven</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1497</span><span class="rf-text">He takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> after a seven-month siege, then holds it for only 100 days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text">He crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, which he rules until 1526</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1511</span><span class="rf-text">With the help of Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> he takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> for the third time, losing it again in 1512</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> and occupies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1527</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Mewar at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1529</span><span class="rf-text">His victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a> ends organised Afghan resistance</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1530</span><span class="rf-text">Babur dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and is succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babur was born Zahīr ud-Dīn Muhammad on 14 February 1483 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andijan">Andijan</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. He was the eldest son of Umar Shaikh Mirza II, the Timurid ruler of Fergana and a great-great-great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, and of Qutlugh Nigar Khanum, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. His names were chosen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Khwaja Ahrar, his father's spiritual master; the nickname Babur, generally taken from the Persian word for tiger or panther, may have spread because his Turco-Mongol soldiers found his given name hard to pronounce.</p><p>His family belonged to the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a> tribe, of Mongol origin, which had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> centuries earlier and taken up the Turco-Persian tradition. His first language was Chagatai Turkic, and he was equally fluent in Classical Persian, the language of the Timurid elite. The lands around Fergana were held by relatives descended from Timur or Genghis Khan who were constantly at war, and rival princes fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> to the west. The main source for his life is his own memoir, the Baburnama.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babur&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Consorts, Issue:</p><ul><li>Stephen Frederic Dale, The garden of the eight paradises: Bābur and the culture of Empire in Central Asia, Afghanistan and India (1483–1530) (Brill, 2004)</li><li>Stephen F. Dale, Babur:Timurid Prince and Mughal Emperor, 1483-1530 (Cambridge University Press, 2018)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1494, aged eleven, Babur succeeded his father, who died when a dovecote collapsed into a ravine. Two hostile uncles and nobles who favoured his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> threatened him, and he kept the throne mainly through his grandmother Aisan Daulat Begum. In 1497 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> after a seven-month siege, but a revolt at home cost him Fergana, and he held Samarkand only 100 days. In 1500-1501 he took it again, was besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>, Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, and had to give his sister Khanzada to Shaybani in marriage to leave in safety. By 1502 he had lost Fergana too and wandered the mountains, writing of the poverty and humiliation he endured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>.</p><p>In October 1504 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> chieftains. After a stay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, where he came to know the work of the poet Mir Ali Shir Nava'i, he took the title of Padshah among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>. When Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> killed Shaybani in 1510, Babur accepted Safavid suzerainty in return for help and took Samarkand for the third time in 1511, losing it to the Uzbeks in 1512. He then trained his army, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> in 1519, Badakhshan in 1521 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in 1522.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Babur claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> as the legacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and received invitations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-khan-lodi">Daulat Khan Lodi</a>, Governor of Punjab, and from Ala-ud-Din, uncle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>. On 21 April 1526, at Panipat, he met Ibrahim's army of about 100,000 soldiers and 1,000 elephants. He lashed together some 700 carts in the Ottoman fashion, placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a>-men and field guns behind them under the Ottoman gunners Ustad Ali Quli and Mustafa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, and encircled the enemy. Ibrahim Lodi died in the battle, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi dynasty</a>, and Babur occupied Delhi, Gwalior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>.</p><p>On 16 March 1527 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Mewar and his coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Afghan chiefs at Khanwa, one of the first battles in India to feature <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>; K. V. Krishna Rao credits his "superior generalship". He took Chanderi in January 1528 and in May 1529 defeated the Afghan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> coalition at the Ghaghra. He kept Agra as his capital, gave the conquered districts to his commanders as revenue assignments and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> and much of the Lodi fiscal system in place. He gave away the treasure he took, and his daughter Gulbadan Begum recalled that every person in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babur&#8221; cites where it covers Administration and the Indian empire, Foreign relations and policies, Religious policy, Literary works:</p><ul><li>Stephen F. Dale, The Garden of the Eight Paradises: Bābur and the Culture of Empire in Central Asia, Afghanistan and India (1483–1530) (Brill, 2004)</li><li>Stephen Frederic Dale, The garden of the eight paradises: Bābur and the culture of Empire in Central Asia, Afghanistan and India (1483–1530) (Brill, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babur's rivals were first his own kin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>, who repeatedly drove him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a>, who regarded him as a foreigner ruling in India, Medini Rai of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and the Afghan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> loyalists. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-khan-lodi">Daulat Khan Lodi</a>'s forces were routed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1524, Babur burned the city for two days. Before Khanwa he denounced Rana Sanga as a kafir, proclaimed the fight a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> and afterwards took the title of ghazi. At Chanderi in 1528 the defenders' women and children immolated themselves in a jauhar, and Babur wrote no word of admiration for his enemy.</p><p>According to the Baburnama, his campaign in northwest India targeted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> as well as apostates, an immense number were killed, and his camps built "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-skulls">towers of skulls</a> of the infidels". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> witnessed this violence and commented on it in four hymns, and historians suggest the violence of the early Mughal period contributed to Sikhism's turn from pacifism to militancy for self-defence. In modern India many Hindu nationalists target Babur as the reputed builder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, accusing him of demolishing a temple for it; in 2019 the Indian Supreme Court held that nothing proves the earlier structure was demolished to build the mosque.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In his last months Babur was preoccupied with the succession. When his eldest son and heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> fell dangerously ill at Sambhal in 1530, Babur, by a celebrated account preserved by Gulbadan Begum, walked three times round the sick-bed and prayed that the illness pass to him. Humayun recovered and Babur's health declined soon afterwards. In a secret will of 1529 he advised Humayun to administer justice according to the ways of every religion, to avoid the sacrifice of the cow, not to ruin the temples of any law-obeying community and to overlook the quarrels of Shias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a>. He divided his lands between Humayun and Kamran in roughly a six-to-five ratio and urged Humayun to deal gently with his brothers.</p><p>Babur died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> on 26 December 1530, aged 47, and Humayun succeeded him. He was first buried at Chauburji in Agra; as he had wished, his remains were later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and reburied in Bagh-e Babur sometime between 1539 and 1544. The realm he left reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> and Kabul across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the Gangetic plain to the frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, but it remained a loose military conquest; as Asher writes, Humayun inherited "a shaky and as yet unconsolidated empire".</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babur&#8221; cites where it covers Death and legacy:</p><ul><li>Stephen Frederic Dale, The garden of the eight paradises: Bābur and the culture of Empire in Central Asia, Afghanistan and India (1483–1530) (Brill, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babur's lasting contribution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was military: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> and mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> tactics taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and from Central Asian warfare. He introduced the symmetrical four-part charbagh garden to the plains of northern India, which became an enduring trademark of Mughal architecture. F. Lehmann in the Encyclopaedia Iranica holds him largely responsible for the spread of Persian cultural influence in the subcontinent through his descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><p>The Baburnama, written in Chagatai Turkic and translated into Persian for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s library by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rahim-khan-i-khanan">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</a> in 1589, is regarded as one of the earliest true autobiographies in Islamic literature; Henry Beveridge ranked it with the confessions of Augustine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, and Stephen Dale describes its author as "relentlessly ambitious, humorous, casually violent" and "highly cultured". Mirza Haidar ranked Babur a Turki poet "second only to Mir Ali Shir". Soviet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> sources regard him as an ethnic Uzbek, an anachronism; he is a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, where many of his poems became folk songs, and some sources say in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> too. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> named its Babur Cruise Missile after him in 2005, while in India the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a>, demolished in December 1992 amid rioting that killed more than a thousand people, keeps his name at the centre of political dispute.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babur&#8221; cites where it covers Death and legacy:</p><ul><li>Stephen Frederic Dale, The garden of the eight paradises: Bābur and the culture of Empire in Central Asia, Afghanistan and India (1483–1530) (Brill, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Babur, a Timurid prince who lost Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> to his rivals, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> his kingdom in 1504 and in 1526-1529 conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with artillery and field tactics learned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. His campaigns were violent, as his own memoir records, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> an unconsolidated realm, but also the Baburnama, his poetry and the charbagh garden.</em></p>

